{"subjectId":"python","id":"advanced-decorators","title":"Advanced Decorators","group":"Functions","order":30,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Beyond the basic wrapper: decorators with optional arguments, decorators that are classes, decorators <em>for</em> classes, registries, and the traps to avoid.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>Recap: the three shapes</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Usage</th><th>What Python calls</th><th>Your decorator must be</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>@d</code></td><td><code>f = d(f)</code></td><td>a callable taking the function</td></tr><tr><td><code>@d(3)</code></td><td><code>f = d(3)(f)</code></td><td>a factory returning a decorator</td></tr><tr><td><code>@d</code> on a class</td><td><code>C = d(C)</code></td><td>a callable taking the class</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Optional arguments: <code>@d</code> and <code>@d(…)</code></h3>\n<p>Make the function the first, optional positional parameter and the options keyword-only. If a function arrives, decorate it now; otherwise return a decorator.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools\n\ndef repeat(func=None, *, times=2):\n    def decorator(f):\n        @functools.wraps(f)\n        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            return [f(*args, **kwargs) for _ in range(times)]\n        return wrapper\n    if func is None:            # used as @repeat(times=3)\n        return decorator\n    return decorator(func)      # used as bare @repeat\n\n@repeat\ndef hi():\n    return \"hi\"\n\n@repeat(times=3)\ndef yo():\n    return \"yo\"\n\nprint(hi(), yo())   # ['hi', 'hi'] ['yo', 'yo', 'yo']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Class-based decorators with state</h3>\n<p>Any callable can be a decorator. A class whose <code>__init__</code> takes the function and whose <code>__call__</code> runs it keeps state naturally in attributes.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools\n\nclass CountCalls:\n    def __init__(self, func):\n        functools.update_wrapper(self, func)   # copy __name__, __doc__\n        self.func = func\n        self.calls = 0\n\n    def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        self.calls += 1\n        return self.func(*args, **kwargs)\n\n@CountCalls\ndef add(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Add two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a + b\n\nadd(1, 2); add(3, 4)\nprint(add.calls, add.__name__, add(5, 5))   # 2 add 10</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A class-based decorator applied to a <strong>method</strong> breaks <code>self</code>: instances of <code>CountCalls</code> aren't descriptors, so <code>obj.method</code> doesn't bind. Add a <code>__get__</code> returning <code>functools.partial(self.__call__, obj)</code>, or use a function-based decorator for methods.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-user\"></i>Decorating methods</h3>\n<p>Function-based decorators work on methods unchanged — <code>self</code> simply arrives as the first element of <code>*args</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools\n\ndef require_login(method):\n    @functools.wraps(method)\n    def wrapper(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        if not self.logged_in:\n            raise PermissionError(f\"{method.__name__} needs login\")\n        return method(self, *args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\nclass Account:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.logged_in = False\n    @require_login\n    def balance(self):\n        return 100\n\nacc = Account()\ntry:\n    acc.balance()\nexcept PermissionError as e:\n    print(e)                  # balance needs login\nacc.logged_in = True\nprint(acc.balance())          # 100</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Class decorators</h3>\n<p>A class decorator receives the finished class and returns it (modified) or a replacement. <code>@dataclass</code> is the famous one — it reads the annotations and generates <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code> and <code>__eq__</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def add_repr(cls):\n    def __repr__(self):\n        fields = \", \".join(f\"{k}={v!r}\" for k, v in vars(self).items())\n        return f\"{cls.__name__}({fields})\"\n    cls.__repr__ = __repr__\n    return cls\n\ndef singleton(cls):\n    instances = {}\n    def get(*args, **kwargs):\n        if cls not in instances:\n            instances[cls] = cls(*args, **kwargs)\n        return instances[cls]\n    return get\n\n@add_repr\nclass User:\n    def __init__(self, name, age):\n        self.name, self.age = name, age\n\n@singleton\nclass Config:\n    pass\n\nprint(User(\"asha\", 30))           # User(name='asha', age=30)\nprint(Config() is Config())       # True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>The <code>singleton</code> above replaces the class with a function, so <code>isinstance(x, Config)</code> no longer works. Decorators that <em>return the class</em> are safer than ones that replace it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plug\"></i>Registries &amp; plugins</h3>\n<p>A decorator doesn't have to wrap anything — it can just <strong>record</strong> the function and return it unchanged. This is how Flask routes, pytest fixtures and CLI command groups work.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>HANDLERS = {}\n\ndef on(event):\n    def register(func):\n        HANDLERS[event] = func\n        return func               # unchanged\n    return register\n\n@on(\"signup\")\ndef welcome(user):\n    return f\"welcome {user}\"\n\n@on(\"delete\")\ndef goodbye(user):\n    return f\"bye {user}\"\n\ndef dispatch(event, user):\n    return HANDLERS[event](user)\n\nprint(sorted(HANDLERS))           # ['delete', 'signup']\nprint(dispatch(\"signup\", \"asha\")) # welcome asha</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack\"></i>Stacking order</h3>\n<p>Decorators are <strong>applied</strong> bottom-up (closest to <code>def</code> first) but the wrappers <strong>run</strong> top-down, like layers of an onion.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools\n\ndef layer(name):\n    def deco(f):\n        print(f\"apply {name}\")\n        @functools.wraps(f)\n        def wrapper():\n            print(f\"enter {name}\")\n            result = f()\n            print(f\"exit {name}\")\n            return result\n        return wrapper\n    return deco\n\n@layer(\"outer\")\n@layer(\"inner\")\ndef work():\n    print(\"work\")\n\nwork()</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>apply inner\napply outer\nenter outer\nenter inner\nwork\nexit inner\nexit outer</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Order matters: <code>@app.route</code> above <code>@login_required</code> protects the route; the other way round registers the <em>unprotected</em> function. Similarly put <code>@cache</code> where you want the cache to sit relative to logging or timing.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i>Validation from type hints</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import functools, inspect\n\ndef enforce_types(func):\n    sig = inspect.signature(func)\n    @functools.wraps(func)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        bound = sig.bind(*args, **kwargs)\n        for name, value in bound.arguments.items():\n            expected = func.__annotations__.get(name)\n            if isinstance(expected, type) and not isinstance(value, expected):\n                raise TypeError(f\"{name} must be {expected.__name__}\")\n        return func(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapper\n\n@enforce_types\ndef repeat_text(text: str, n: int) -&gt; str:\n    return text * n\n\nprint(repeat_text(\"ab\", 2))           # abab\ntry:\n    repeat_text(\"ab\", \"2\")\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)                          # n must be int\nprint(inspect.signature(repeat_text)) # (text: str, n: int) -&gt; str</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>functools.wraps</code> sets <code>__wrapped__</code>, which <code>inspect.signature</code> follows — so the decorated function still shows its real signature in help, IDEs and docs.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-door-exit\"></i>Decorators that are also context managers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from contextlib import ContextDecorator\n\nclass traced(ContextDecorator):\n    def __init__(self, label):\n        self.label = label\n    def __enter__(self):\n        print(f\"start {self.label}\")\n        return self\n    def __exit__(self, *exc):\n        print(f\"end {self.label}\")\n        return False\n\n@traced(\"job\")                    # as a decorator\ndef job():\n    print(\"working\")\n\njob()\nwith traced(\"block\"):             # as a with-statement\n    print(\"inline\")</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>start job\nworking\nend job\nstart block\ninline\nend block</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pitfalls</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag-off\"></i>Forgetting <code>wraps</code></h3><p>Name, docstring and signature are lost — confusing tracebacks, broken introspection.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back\"></i>Forgetting to return</h3><p>The wrapper must <code>return func(...)</code>, and the decorator must <code>return wrapper</code> — otherwise the function becomes <code>None</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i><code>@d</code> vs <code>@d()</code></h3><p>A factory used without parentheses receives the function as its option argument — support both forms or be consistent.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Import-time side effects</h3><p>Decorator bodies run when the module is imported. Keep them cheap; do the work inside the wrapper.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you write a decorator that accepts arguments?</summary><div><p>Add a level: an outer function takes the arguments and returns the actual decorator, which takes the function and returns the wrapper. <code>@retry(times=3)</code> means <code>f = retry(times=3)(f)</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a class decorator? Give an example.</summary><div><p>A callable that receives a class and returns a class (usually the same one, modified). <code>@dataclass</code> inspects the annotations and adds <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code> and <code>__eq__</code>; <code>@functools.total_ordering</code> adds comparison methods.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>In what order are stacked decorators applied?</summary><div><p>Bottom-up: <code>@a @b def f</code> is <code>f = a(b(f))</code>. At call time the outermost wrapper (<code>a</code>) runs first and calls into <code>b</code>'s wrapper, which calls <code>f</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}